WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.120
Okay.

00:04.120 --> 00:06.720
So now let's have a quick summary about the set operators.

00:06.720 --> 00:12.080
So the set operator is going to go and combine the rows of multiple queries multiple tables into one

00:12.080 --> 00:12.960
single results.

00:12.960 --> 00:15.480
And we have four different types of the set operators.

00:15.480 --> 00:20.640
The first one is the union where it's going to go and combine all the rows, but without including any

00:20.640 --> 00:21.520
duplicates.

00:21.520 --> 00:23.360
The second one we have the Union all.

00:23.400 --> 00:26.000
It's very similar, and the third one we have the accepts.

00:26.040 --> 00:31.040
It's going to show all the rows from the first query that cannot be found in the second query.

00:31.080 --> 00:35.760
And the fourth one we have the intersect where it's going to show the common rows between two queries.

00:35.800 --> 00:37.120
And of course we have SQL rules.

00:37.120 --> 00:42.360
In order to use the set operators, both of the queries should have the same number of columns, the

00:42.400 --> 00:45.120
same data types, and the order of columns.

00:45.160 --> 00:45.920
And the last rule.

00:45.920 --> 00:51.800
Don't forget that the first query controls the aliases, the name of the columns, and the data types

00:51.800 --> 00:53.240
of the entire result.

00:53.240 --> 00:58.080
And we have found amazing use cases for the set operators, like for example using union and union,

00:58.120 --> 01:02.710
all in order to combine similar informations Formations into one big table.

01:02.710 --> 01:04.990
Or we can go and use the amazing except.

01:05.030 --> 01:05.590
Operator.

01:05.750 --> 01:08.950
In order to compare two different results in order to find.

01:08.990 --> 01:14.710
The differences between them, and I usually use it in order to do data quality checks to test the data

01:14.710 --> 01:15.590
completeness.

01:15.590 --> 01:16.590
And another use case.

01:16.590 --> 01:22.310
As a data engineer, you can go and implement the excepts in your logic in your data pipelines in order

01:22.310 --> 01:26.470
to identify what are the new data that must be inserted in your system.

01:26.510 --> 01:27.190
Okay my friends.

01:27.190 --> 01:30.870
So with that, we have learned all the set operators that we have inside SQL.

01:30.870 --> 01:35.270
And with that, you have learned how to combine your data from multiple tables using SQL.

01:35.310 --> 01:36.870
So we are done with this chapter.

01:36.910 --> 01:38.790
Now we're going to go to the right side.

01:38.790 --> 01:41.790
So now we're going to start talking about the functions in SQL.

01:41.830 --> 01:43.590
And here we have two big families.

01:43.590 --> 01:47.230
The first one is the row level or the single value functions.

01:47.230 --> 01:49.990
And the second one we have the aggregate analytical functions.

01:49.990 --> 01:52.790
So let's start with the first one the row level functions.

01:52.790 --> 01:55.150
And here we can group them into multiple categories.

01:55.150 --> 01:57.350
And we will start now with the string functions.

01:57.350 --> 02:01.910
But first let's understand what is exactly functions and why do we need them in SQL.

02:01.950 --> 02:02.830
So let's go.
